[{"category":"section","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis","page":"Home","text":"Multi-spacecraft analysis methods in Julia.\n\n(Image: DOI) (Image: version)","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reciprocal-vectors","page":"Home","text":"Paschmann and Daly [1], Chapter 4, Paschmann and Daly [2], Chapter 14, ","title":"Reciprocal vectors"},{"category":"section","location":"#Estimation-of-spatial-gradients","page":"Home","text":"Since tildeg and tildeboldsymbolV are linear functions, the calculation of spatial derivatives, such as the gradient of some scalar function or the divergence or curl of a vector function, can be done quite easily. The results are:\n\nbeginaligned\nnabla g simeq nabla tildeg  =sum_alpha=0^3 boldsymbolk_alpha g_alpha \nhatboldsymbole cdot nabla g simeq hatboldsymbole cdot nabla tildeg  =sum_alpha=0^3left(hatboldsymbole cdot boldsymbolk_alpharight) g_alpha \nnabla cdot boldsymbolV simeq nabla cdot tildeboldsymbolV  =sum_alpha=0^3 boldsymbolk_alpha cdot boldsymbolV_alpha \nnabla times boldsymbolV simeq nabla times tildeboldsymbolV  =sum_alpha=0^3 boldsymbolk_alpha times boldsymbolV_alpha\nendaligned","title":"Estimation of spatial gradients"},{"category":"section","location":"#Multi-spacecraft-timing","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Multi-spacecraft timing"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"Home","text":"\n\nG. Paschmann and P. W. Daly. Multi-Spacecraft Analysis Methods Revisited (ESA Communications, 2008).\n\n\n\nG. Paschmann and P. W. Daly. Analysis Methods for Multi-Spacecraft Data (ESA Publications Division, 2000).\n\n\n\n","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vector","page":"Home","text":"reciprocal_vector(r_βα, r_βγ, r_βλ)\n\nCompute the reciprocal vector 𝒌_α for a vertex of a tetrahedron given the relative position vectors.\n\n𝒌_α = frac𝐫_βγ  𝐫_βλ𝐫_βα  (𝐫_βγ  𝐫_βλ)\n\nwhere 𝐫_αβ = r_β - r_α are relative position vectors.\n\nReferences\n\nMulti-spacecraft analysis methods revisited : 4.3 Properties of reciprocal vectors\n\n\n\n\n\nreciprocal_vector(rα, rβ, rγ, rλ)\n\nCompute the reciprocal vector 𝒌_α for a vertex of a tetrahedron given the position vectors of all vertices.\n\nThe vertices (α, β, γ, λ) must form a cyclic permutation of (1, 2, 3, 4).\n\n\n\n\n\nreciprocal_vector(rα, r0s)\n\nGeneralised reciprocal vector for N != 4\n\n𝐪_α = 𝐑^-1 𝐫_α\n\nSee also: reciprocal_vector, position_tensor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vector"},{"category":"function","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vectors","page":"Home","text":"Compute the set of reciprocal vectors {𝒌_α}, which is also called the reciprocal base of the tetrahedron.\n\nSee also: reciprocal_vector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vectors"},{"category":"function","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.position_tensor","page":"Home","text":"𝐑 = _α (𝐫_α-𝐫_b) (𝐫_α-𝐫_b) = _α 𝐫_α 𝐫_α-𝐫_b 𝐫_b\n\nwith 𝐫_b = _α 𝐫_α  N and N is the number of positions.\n\nReferences\n\nPaschmann and Daly [1] Paschmann & Daly, 2008. Section 4.7\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.position_tensor"},{"category":"function","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.volumetric_tensor","page":"Home","text":"volumetric_tensor(rs::AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector})\n\nfrac1N 𝐑.\n\nSee also: position_tensor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.volumetric_tensor"},{"category":"function","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.tetrahedron_quality","page":"Home","text":"Calculate tetrahedron quality factors\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.tetrahedron_quality"},{"category":"function","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.lingradest","page":"Home","text":"lingradest(B1, B2, B3, B4, R1, R2, R3, R4)\n\nCompute spatial derivatives such as grad, div, curl and curvature using reciprocal vector technique (linear interpolation).\n\nArguments\n\nB1, B2, B3, B4: 3-element vectors giving magnetic field measurements at each probe\nR1, R2, R3, R4: 3-element vectors giving the probe positions\n\nReturns\n\nA named tuple containing:   • Rbary: Barycenter position   • Bbc: Magnetic field at the barycenter   • Bmag: Magnetic field magnitude at the barycenter   • LGBx, LGBy, LGBz: Linear gradient estimators for each component   • div: Linear divergence estimator   • curl: Linear curl estimator   • curv: Field-line curvature vector: 𝐛 · ∇𝐛   • R_c: Field-line curvature radius\n\nReferences\n\nBased on the method of Chanteur (ISSI, 1998, Ch. 11).\n\nlingradest.pro\nlingradest.py\n\n\n\n\n\nlingradest(B1::AbstractMatrix, args...; dim = 1, flatten = false, select = nothing)\n\nVectorized method for simplified usage. Returns a StructArray containing the results.\n\nSet flatten = true to flatten the output arrays, making the output shape similar to the input array shape.\n\nPass select = (:field1, :field2, ...) to materialize only the listed columns.\n\n\n\n\n\nlingradest(\n    Bx1, Bx2, Bx3, Bx4,\n    By1, By2, By3, By4,\n    Bz1, Bz2, Bz3, Bz4,\n    R1, R2, R3, R4\n)\n\nSPEDAS-argument-compatible version of lingradest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.lingradest"},{"category":"function","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.ConstantVelocityApproach","page":"Home","text":"CVA(positions, times)\n\nConstant Velocity Approach (CVA) for determining boundary normal and velocity. Solve timing equation: D * m = Δts\n\nParameters:\n\npositions: Positions of 4 spacecraft (4×3 array)\ntimes: Times of boundary crossing for each spacecraft\n\n\n\n\n\nConstantVelocityApproach(positions, times, durations)\n\nGiven durations of the boundary crossings, calculate the thickness of the boundary\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.ConstantVelocityApproach"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.ConstantThicknessApproach-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"Constant Thickness Approach (CTA) for determining boundary normal and velocity. Based on the method described in Haaland et al., Annales Geophysicae, 2004.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.ConstantThicknessApproach"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.ConstantVelocityApproach-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"ConstantVelocityApproach(positions, times, durations)\n\nGiven durations of the boundary crossings, calculate the thickness of the boundary\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.ConstantVelocityApproach"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.ConstantVelocityApproach-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"CVA(positions, times)\n\nConstant Velocity Approach (CVA) for determining boundary normal and velocity. Solve timing equation: D * m = Δts\n\nParameters:\n\npositions: Positions of 4 spacecraft (4×3 array)\ntimes: Times of boundary crossing for each spacecraft\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.ConstantVelocityApproach"},{"category":"function","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.DiscontinuityAnalyzer","page":"Home","text":"Discontinuity Analyzer (DA) for analyzing properties of discontinuities using multi-spacecraft measurements.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.DiscontinuityAnalyzer"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.lingradest-NTuple{16, Any}","page":"Home","text":"lingradest(\n    Bx1, Bx2, Bx3, Bx4,\n    By1, By2, By3, By4,\n    Bz1, Bz2, Bz3, Bz4,\n    R1, R2, R3, R4\n)\n\nSPEDAS-argument-compatible version of lingradest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.lingradest"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.lingradest-NTuple{8, Any}","page":"Home","text":"lingradest(B1, B2, B3, B4, R1, R2, R3, R4)\n\nCompute spatial derivatives such as grad, div, curl and curvature using reciprocal vector technique (linear interpolation).\n\nArguments\n\nB1, B2, B3, B4: 3-element vectors giving magnetic field measurements at each probe\nR1, R2, R3, R4: 3-element vectors giving the probe positions\n\nReturns\n\nA named tuple containing:   • Rbary: Barycenter position   • Bbc: Magnetic field at the barycenter   • Bmag: Magnetic field magnitude at the barycenter   • LGBx, LGBy, LGBz: Linear gradient estimators for each component   • div: Linear divergence estimator   • curl: Linear curl estimator   • curv: Field-line curvature vector: 𝐛 · ∇𝐛   • R_c: Field-line curvature radius\n\nReferences\n\nBased on the method of Chanteur (ISSI, 1998, Ch. 11).\n\nlingradest.pro\nlingradest.py\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.lingradest"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.lingradest-Tuple{Vararg{AbstractMatrix}}","page":"Home","text":"lingradest(B1::AbstractMatrix, args...; dim = 1, flatten = false, select = nothing)\n\nVectorized method for simplified usage. Returns a StructArray containing the results.\n\nSet flatten = true to flatten the output arrays, making the output shape similar to the input array shape.\n\nPass select = (:field1, :field2, ...) to materialize only the listed columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.lingradest"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.m2nV-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","text":"Convert slowness vector 𝐦 = 𝐧V to normal vector and velocity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.m2nV"},{"category":"function","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.position_tensor-2","page":"Home","text":"𝐑 = _α (𝐫_α-𝐫_b) (𝐫_α-𝐫_b) = _α 𝐫_α 𝐫_α-𝐫_b 𝐫_b\n\nwith 𝐫_b = _α 𝐫_α  N and N is the number of positions.\n\nReferences\n\nPaschmann and Daly [1] Paschmann & Daly, 2008. Section 4.7\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.position_tensor"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vector-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Home","text":"reciprocal_vector(rα, rβ, rγ, rλ)\n\nCompute the reciprocal vector 𝒌_α for a vertex of a tetrahedron given the position vectors of all vertices.\n\nThe vertices (α, β, γ, λ) must form a cyclic permutation of (1, 2, 3, 4).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vector-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"reciprocal_vector(r_βα, r_βγ, r_βλ)\n\nCompute the reciprocal vector 𝒌_α for a vertex of a tetrahedron given the relative position vectors.\n\n𝒌_α = frac𝐫_βγ  𝐫_βλ𝐫_βα  (𝐫_βγ  𝐫_βλ)\n\nwhere 𝐫_αβ = r_β - r_α are relative position vectors.\n\nReferences\n\nMulti-spacecraft analysis methods revisited : 4.3 Properties of reciprocal vectors\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vector-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"reciprocal_vector(rα, r0s)\n\nGeneralised reciprocal vector for N != 4\n\n𝐪_α = 𝐑^-1 𝐫_α\n\nSee also: reciprocal_vector, position_tensor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vectors-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Home","text":"Compute the set of reciprocal vectors {𝒌_α}, which is also called the reciprocal base of the tetrahedron.\n\nSee also: reciprocal_vector\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.reciprocal_vectors"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.tetrahedron_quality-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector}}","page":"Home","text":"Calculate tetrahedron quality factors\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.tetrahedron_quality"},{"category":"method","location":"#MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.volumetric_tensor-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector}}","page":"Home","text":"volumetric_tensor(rs::AbstractVector{<:AbstractVector})\n\nfrac1N 𝐑.\n\nSee also: position_tensor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MultiSpacecraftAnalysis.volumetric_tensor"}]
